title: Token budgets
summary: Sections of different sizes, a rule that must be carried and open tasks, read with budgets set exactly at the size of a view and one token under it, so what a budget keeps and omits stays as it is when budget counting is refactored.
status: draft
---

## Goal

Ship the refund service before the end of the quarter, with the rules below carried into every read.

:::constraint{#TB-C1 applies-to=TB-R1} Every refund is logged with the order number
:::

## Requirements

:::requirement{#TB-R1} A refund needs the order number
The order number is checked against the order service before any money moves.
:::

:::requirement{#TB-R2} Refunds over 500 need a second approval
:::

## Plan

- [ ] Build the refund endpoint {#TB-T1}
- [ ] Add the approval step :depends[TB-T1] {#TB-T2}
- [x] Agree the limits with finance {#TB-T3}

## Background

The old refund process ran on a spreadsheet. Each request was copied by hand into the payment tool, checked by a
second person and then paid. Requests often waited a week, and the order number was sometimes typed wrong, so the
wrong customer was refunded twice last year. The new service reads the order directly, so nobody types it.

The finance team asked for an audit trail, a second approval above a limit and a daily report. The audit trail is the
log the constraint asks for. The report is out of scope for the first release and comes later.

## History

- 2026-09-01. The spreadsheet process was reviewed.
- 2026-09-15. Finance agreed the 500 limit.
- 2026-10-01. Work on the service started.
- 2026-10-20. The first endpoint was demonstrated.

## Notes for people

:::human
Ask Maya before changing the approval limit. She owns the agreement with finance.
:::
